      "isRC": true,
      "passage": "Directions for questions 5 to 8: The passage below is accompanied by a set of questions. Choose\nthe best answer to each question.\nThe disappearance of mammoth, mastodon, woolly rhino, great sloths, or sabre-tooth \u2018tigers\u2019 from the\nHolarctic fauna at the end of the Pleistocene continues to intrigue. . . Species extinctions are a grim\nreality of recent centuries and can be attributed to chemical impacts, habitat destruction and harassment,\npredation by overhunting, or deliberate extermination. Some such extinctions or near-extinctions were\nrapid; others followed many centuries of sustained attrition. But for Late Pleistocene megafaunal extinctions, there is neither direct testimony nor a promising analytical method to identify cause and effect, such as\nfor the demise of the carrier pigeon, or the near-extinction of bison, bald eagle, or whooping crane. . . .\nThe case of extinctions in Australia makes the point. Between perhaps 30,000 and 14,000 BP(Before\nPresent), a total of 42 larger vertebrates, mainly marsupials and several taxa, were subject to size reduction.\nTwo of the vertebrate families that disappeared were huge, clumsy and slow-moving browsers, reminiscent\nof large mammals of the early Tertiary. The kangaroo family (Macropodidae) lost six genera (or 30 species),\nbut it could be argued that the surviving kangaroos and wallaby (10 species) are \u2018modernised,\u2019 efficient,\nand smaller forms of the same lineages (phyletic replacement). In the big picture, this suggests the\nconsequence of intergeneric\u2014 different types of animals from separate groups compete\u2014 and interspecific\ncompetition\u2014 when different species within the same group compete.\nThere also were several major environmental discontinuities during the same time span, including filling\nand drying of lakes, expansion and \u2018downsizing\u2019 of river systems, and extensive activation of eolian\nsands. . . . Pollen profiles suggest re-expansion of browsing habitats about 10,000 BP. . . . In combination\nwith the sudden, drastic climatic perturbations for which Australia is noted today, that implies repeated\nepisodes of fragmentation and reduction of habitats during the Late Pleistocene, rather than a single and\nunidirectional change at its close. Environmental flux of sufficient amplitude would selectively disadvantage\nthe demographic viability of \u2018archaic\u2019 forms, accelerating competitive exclusion among genera with overlapping\nniches.\nPeople settled in parts of Australia before 40,000 BP, but they were few in number, their distribution was\nlimited to more productive environments, and they introduced no animals other than the dingo at a much\nlater date. . . . Artifacts have been found at a handful of faunal sites that lack patent evidence of butchering\nactivity. How does one link the coexistence of human hunters or scavengers with extinctions over a time\nrange of 25 millennia? Alternatively, how does one distinguish an evolutionary turn over from human\npredation? Temporal correlations do not provide process-links. . .\nUnlike Australia. . ., the contiguous continents of the Old World were little affected by extinctions . . .: a\nfew phyletic replacements\u2014 replacement of one species by another within a lineage\u2014 here and there,\nand the more dramatic disappearance of elephant and rhinoceros in Palearctic Eurasia for the first time\nsince the Tertiary. Unimpeded contact and faunal interchange in the Old World imply a steady intergeneric\ncompetition for overlapping niches, which may have driven Old World faunas to modernise earlier. As on\nthe other continents, that does not exclude human predation as a triggering mechanism for the extinction\nof some vulnerable taxa. But neither does the present evidence single out humans as super-predators ten\nmillennia ago, whatever the environmental inputs and biological feedbacks of end-Pleistocene extinctions."
