      "passage": "Directions for questions 9 to 12: The passage below is accompanied by a set of questions.\nChoose the best answer to each question.\nThe digitization of human knowledge and the emergence of machine learning have led to the rise of data\nscience as a discipline. The accumulation of data has allowed machines to teach themselves by detecting\npatterns. The tools developed for intelligence and counterintelligence purposes during the Cold War, such\nas traffic analysis and anomaly detection, have contributed to the development of data science. National\nfunding agencies shifted their priorities, and economics and political science became predictive sciences\nbased on data analysis, framing general theories, and attempts to verify them. This approach has had a\ndistorting effect on other fields, such as philosophy and political theory, which atrophied.\nTechnocracy, a political movement that emerged in the 1930s, proposed the application of science to the\nsocial order. Technocrats aimed to abolish all existing economic and political arrangements, such as\ngovernments and banks, and replace them with engineers who would rule by numbers. This vision of\ntechnological supremacy found its fullest expression in the Technocracy movement, which vied with\nsocialism and fascism as an alternative to capitalism and liberal democracy. The move from a culture of\nnumbers to a culture of data began during the Second World War when statistics became more\nmathematical, largely for the sake of becoming more predictive, necessary for wartime applications involving\neverything from calculating missile trajectories to cracking codes.\nWhile the emergence of data science as a discipline is thrilling, the worst of the ills produced by the\nhubristic enthusiasm for data today most often have to do with making predictions about human behaviour\nand apportioning resources accordingly. The use of algorithms to set bail or sentences for people accused\nor convicted of crimes is one example. There is a risk of presenting data science as a master discipline,\ncapable of reorienting the sciences, the commercial world, and governance itself. Legal scholars have\nproposed writing better algorithms to solve biases in algorithms, but the result is still ruled by algorithms.\nThe solution to the problem may be to stop clinging to the raft of data, return to the ocean of mystery, and\ngo fishing for facts.\nThe emergence of data science as a distinct field has resulted in an explosion of data-science-focused\ncoursework, as well as institutes and even entire educational institutions. One of the job categories that\nis expanding at the quickest rate right now in the United States is that of the data scientist. However, one\nof the risks associated with data science is that it has the potential to be portrayed as a master discipline\nthat is able to reorient not only the scientific community but also the business world and governance\nitself. In the worst possible scenarios, data science can lead to arrogance and other problems that are\ncaused by an excessive fixation on numbers. One illustration of this would be the application of algorithms\nto forecast human behaviour and divide up available resources. History is not a science that can predict\nthe future; if it could, it would no longer be considered history."
