      "passage": "Directions for questions 13 to 16: The passage below is accompanied by a set of questions.\nChoose the best answer to each question.\nThe sociology of art has long been concerned with how musical taste contributes to the construction of\nsocial differences. Sociologists have explored the historical context of status groups and their musical\npreferences in order to understand how they attempt to establish themselves as arbiters of \u201cgood taste\u201d.\nStudies have also shown how musical values are not \u201cpure\u201d but rather linked to the maintenance of social\ndistinctions. According to Pierre Bourdieu\u2019s Distinction (1984), the social distribution of musical tastes\nreinforces socioeconomic relations. Cultural dispositions are, therefore, a means of social regulation,\nwhich are instilled in the education system.\nStudies of taste and exclusion highlight how the hierarchy of musical works and music producers comes\nto function as a vehicle for social differentiation and exclusion. The sociology of music has explored how\nmusical value is articulated and how reputation can be understood as the outcome of musical \u201cpolitics\u201d.\nA study of Beethoven\u2019s rise to fame and renown during the first ten years of his Viennese career reveals\nhow some come to be hailed as \u201cgreat\u201d while others are seen as existing in the shadow of that greatness.\nThere are two main reasons why the case of Beethoven is sociologically interesting. To begin, it is\ngenerally agreed that his body of work occupies a central position within the musical canon. Second,\nduring the first ten years that Beethoven spent in Vienna, he did not receive universal acclaim from\neveryone. Many people had the impression that he was antithetical to anything that could be considered\nmusically worthy. Sociologists were fascinated by precisely this controversy, as well as the way it eventually\ndiverted attention away from the myth surrounding Beethoven\u2019s talent. Beethoven\u2019s greatness can be traced\nback to the ways in which the concept of greatness itself was reshaped to \u201cfit\u201d the forms Beethoven created.\nGiven that the musical canon is a social construction, it is essential, from the point of view of music\neducation, to have an understanding that the boundaries of musical value are influenced by society.\nThose who teach music in schools need to be aware of this fact and refrain from endorsing values that\nwere constructed into what is regarded as a \u201cgiven\u201d form of excellence, such as the idea that \u201cgreat\u201d\ncomposers are always men.\nThe social recognition of talent, as it was described in the musical world of Vienna during the time of\nBeethoven, is applicable in any musical world, including the world of the classroom. The development of\nmusical talent is understood to be a multifaceted process, one that may or may not conform to preconceived\nnotions of what constitutes \u201cgood practice.\u201d In the classroom, students may be assigned to different\ncategories based on hierarchies of talent and articulations of musical value, but these classifications may\nnot correspond well to the student\u2019s abilities and preferences outside of the classroom. In order for us to\ncomprehend this, we need to concentrate on the unstructured practises of music education because it is\nthrough these practices that both individual and collective identities are shaped."
